    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-traumatic osteoarthritis of the left hindfoot (M19.172)</content>. Good response to the injection, brace and therapy: pain 3 of 10 and he is riding. Arthrodesis was explained again with its benefits, the months of recovery and the effect on riding; he chose to keep his foot as it is and to raise surgery again if pain stops him walking or riding. His decision is respected. [S]<list><item>Continue the brace, home exercises and diclofenac gel; acetaminophen as needed.</item><item>A repeat subtalar injection when needed, no sooner than three months after the first and no more than three or four a year.</item><item>No further oral NSAID for now.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Dyspepsia on naproxen (R10.13)</content>. Resolved after stopping naproxen; H. pylori negative. [S]<list><item>Finish the four weeks of omeprazole, then stop.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Pain on walking (0 to 10 numeric rating scale)</td><td>Every visit and after each treatment trial</td><td>Completed today</td><td>3 of 10, five weeks after the injection</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Function: Foot and Ankle Ability Measure, activities of daily living subscale</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>81%; riding again</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Joint examination: hindfoot and ankle motion, alignment, swelling and gait</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Subtalar motion less painful; no swelling</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Exercise, physical therapy and brace use</td><td>Every visit; physical therapy weekly for six weeks</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Six physical therapy sessions completed, two by video; home exercises most days</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>NSAID safety: blood pressure, creatinine and blood count; stomach symptoms</td><td>Before an oral NSAID, then every 6 to 12 months on long-term use</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Naproxen stopped at the sick visit for dyspepsia; topical diclofenac only; hemoglobin 14.4 g/dL then</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Imaging</td><td>At diagnosis or for surgical planning; not for routine follow-up</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not needed: improving, and no surgery planned</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood lead with retained bullet fragments</td><td>Once when fragments are found; repeat only if fragments lie in a joint or bone, or symptoms suggest lead toxicity</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Fragments outside the joints; no symptoms of lead toxicity</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure, with home readings to confirm or exclude hypertension</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>124/78 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screening, sleep and coping</td><td>At baseline and periodically</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 4, item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hemoglobin A1c in prediabetes</td><td>At diagnosis, then in three to four months and yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Due at the next visit</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Food access and the food distribution program</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Enrolled in the food distribution program last week</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Traditional healing alongside clinic care, at his choice</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>His choice; nothing asked</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Walk a mile on uneven ground and ride with pain of 3 or less. Outcome by 1886-09-14: Pain 7 to 3 of 10 on walking; walking more than a mile with the brace; riding again [S]</item><item>Decide about surgery with full information, in his own time. Outcome by 1886-09-14: Arthrodesis explained with its risks and recovery; he chose to wait and will raise it again if needed [S]</item><item>Bring hemoglobin A1c below 5.7%. In progress; target 1886-10-26 [S]</item><item>Enough food through the month. In progress; target 1886-10-26 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2019 American College of Rheumatology/Arthritis Foundation Guideline for the Management of Osteoarthritis of the Hand, Hip, and Knee (2019); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis and depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monographs (2026); Lead toxicity from retained bullet fragments: A systematic review and meta-analysis (2019); Indian Health Manual, Part 3, Chapter 30: Chronic Non-Cancer Pain Management (2026); Special General Memorandum 94-08: Statement of Policy for the Traditional Cultural Advocacy Program (1994); ACG and CAG Clinical Guideline: Management of Dyspepsia (2017)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive visit next week. Sixteen-week review in six weeks with hemoglobin A1c. Next scheduled visit 1886-09-21: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

